thesis are outlined as follows:

    To the best of our knowledge, the proposed Catfish-effect-based deep reinforcement

      learning (CDRL) framework is the first to address the integration of active RIS with
      element switching control using DRL. CDRL employs two competing DRL agents to
      optimize communication performance.

    In the proposed CDRL approach, we design three core mechanisms: all experiences are

      separated based on an energy efficiency (EE) threshold, the two DRL agents are assigned
      different discount factors to focus on distinct objectives, and the catfish agent is provided
      with an adaptive competitive reward mechanism to promote mutual competition between

      the agents.


