Chapter 2

Research Background

    This section 2.1 describes related work, and section 2.2 discusses research motivation.


2.1     Related works

    Previous research on network transmission rate or energy efficiency has often relied on

mathematical optimization. Under the passive RIS setting, Dejonghe et al. [4] proposed a prac-
tical, low-cost, and high-performance RIS system design based on semi-definite programming
(SDP) to achieve RIS beamforming optimization. In the domain of artificial intelligence–based

approaches, Huang et al. [11] investigated the joint design of BS transmit beamforming and
RIS phase shifts to maximize the sum rate of a multi-user downlink MISO system using
DRL, successfully opening a new research direction for deep reinforcement learning. As a

result, a growing number of studies have explored deep reinforcement learning and other AI-
based solutions. For example, Saglam et al. [12] proposed a novel DRL-based modification
for RIS-assisted systems, highlighting the adaptability and flexibility of deep reinforcement

learning under hardware impairments and limited channel state information. Zhou et al. [15]
introduced an innovative Co-HDRL algorithm for intelligent hierarchical decision-making in
wireless networks to address problems characterized by different time scales.

   The multiplicative fading effect limits the capacity gains of passive RIS in wireless commu-
nication environments, leading to increasing research on active RIS architectures [6–8]. Active
RIS can actively reflect and amplify signals, functioning similarly to relay base stations. Fo-

                                              6
tock et al. [6] considered maximizing energy efficiency (EE) in interference-limited RIS-assisted
wireless networks and compared the performance of active and near-passive RIS. Their devel-

oped algorithm jointly optimizes RIS reflection coefficients, mobile user transmit power, and
the base station’s linear receive filters. Wang et al. [7] applied an alternating optimization
(AO) algorithm based on majorization-minimization (MM) and fractional programming (FP)

methods to solve the problem. The objective is to maximize the achievable downlink rate
under the transmit power constraints of the base station (BS) and the dual active RISs. de
Souza et al. [8] proposed an EE optimization framework for active RIS-assisted massive MIMO

(mMIMO) systems and designed a low-complexity RIS configuration algorithm. They refor-
mulated the problem as an equivalent quadratically constrained quadratic program (QCQP),
from which novel closed-form solutions for the phase and amplitude of each active RIS element

were derived to maximize EE. These results demonstrate that active RIS positively contributes
to enhancing energy efficiency.
   However, a high count of active elements drives up the system’s total energy draw, thereby

lowering energy efficiency (EE). As a result, growing research has focused on element switch-
ing control [19][20]. Under the passive RIS setting, Faisal et al. [19] focused on optimizing
the states of quantized phase shifters and RIS elements using an efficient DRL-based ap-

proach. The agent determines which RIS elements to activate or deactivate based on dynamic
information from cellular networks under varying channel state information and total rate
requirements. Cao et al. [20] studied ON/OFF-based reflection and binary phase-shift key-

ing modulation schemes, developing a new RIS-assisted MISO SR system and a DRL-based
framework to solve the EE maximization problem. In contrast, this thesis investigates the
effect of switching under active RIS conditions [9][10]. Previously, Xie et al. [9] studied and

analyzed the switching mechanism of RIS elements and evaluated its impact on EE perfor-
mance in both passive and active RIS-assisted communication networks. They developed two
algorithms based on alternating optimization (AO), employing variable substitution, quadratic

transformation, and successive convex approximation (SCA) methods to obtain correspond-



                                               7
ing suboptimal solutions. Chen et al. [10] proposed the BPOF scheme, which demonstrates a
beamforming optimization strategy based on communication-element switch control, applied

to EE-optimized RIS-assisted wireless systems.


