        In current wireless communication system research, active reconfigurable intelligent
surfaces (active RIS) are regarded as a key technology to overcome the limitations of traditional

passive RIS in terms of energy efficiency (EE) and transmission performance, due to their
ability to provide active gain. However, the components required for active RIS, such as
radio frequency (RF) amplifiers, introduce additional power consumption, which may prevent

the overall EE of the system from improving. To address this issue, recent studies have
begun exploring the design of active RIS systems incorporating element switching control
mechanisms. By selectively activating or deactivating certain RIS elements, unnecessary power

consumption can be reduced, thereby enhancing EE. Nevertheless, most existing research relies
on mathematical optimization techniques, such as alternating optimization (AO), or successive
convex approximation (SCA) [9, 10]. Although these methods are theoretically sound, they

face challenges in implementation and computational complexity when applied to dynamic
channel environments or high-dimensional control scenarios.
   On the other hand, deep reinforcement learning (DRL) has been proven effective in ad-

dressing RIS element control, phase adjustment, and beamforming without requiring precise
channel models [17]. DRL is particularly suitable for scenarios involving unknown models,
high-dimensional state spaces, and dynamic environments. However, current applications of

DRL in RIS systems remain limited to passive architectures [19, 20], and research on DRL-
based optimization for active RIS combined with element switching control has yet to emerge.
Therefore, this study aims to fill this research gap by proposing a DRL-based optimization

framework for active RIS, which jointly considers RIS element switching control and transmit
beamforming design to effectively improve energy efficiency of the system.
   This thesis considers the same two types of communication system architectures as in

BPOF [10]. They are the switching control of passive RIS–assisted communication system
and the switching control of active RIS–assisted communication system. The former en-
hances transmission efficiency by adjusting the phase shifts of passive reflecting elements and

reduces power consumption by controlling the ON/OFF states of the elements to improve



                                               9
energy efficiency, as illustrated in Fig. 2 (a). The latter further incorporates active amplifier
components to provide additional signal gain, thereby overcoming the multiplicative fading

limitations faced by conventional passive RIS, as illustrated in Fig. 2 (b). By comparing
the performance of these two architectures under different channel conditions and element
switching configurations, this thesis aims to explore the potential benefits of element switch-

ing control in active RIS architectures and to design reinforcement learning algorithms suitable
for high-dimensional continuous action spaces, with the goal of optimizing energy efficiency.




