   After formulating the energy efficiency (EE) maximization problem P0 and P1 , this section

proposes a deep reinforcement learning–based framework to solve it. As illustrated in Fig. 7,
this study introduces an innovative Catfish-effect-based deep reinforcement learning (CDRL)
architecture, which enhances sample efficiency through a dual-agent, dual-replay mechanism.

Inspired by the Catfish effect in management—where a newly introduced competitor stimulates
existing members to perform better—the framework deploys a catfish agent dedicated to
exploring and collecting rare, high-reward experiences in the EE-optimal region, which the

high-reward experience replay buffer holds. In parallel, the main agent interacts with the
environment under general conditions and accumulates data in the general situation experience
replay buffer. At random intervals, the system triggers intervention by injecting high-reward

batches filtered by the catfish agent into the main agent’s training process. This approach
enables experience-level knowledge transfer, which differs from traditional Teacher–Student

                                                              21
Learning based on weight or logit distillation (as shown in Fig. 6). It avoids strategy stagnation
due to over-imitation and preserves policy diversity between the two agents.


