follows:


    The first is a basic actor-critic reinforcement learning model [11] (black line with hollow

      squares) serving as the baseline, with the fully activated RIS variant depicted as a black

      line with hollow triangles.

    The second is the BPOF mathematical analytical solution [10] (blue line with solid dia-

      monds), based on the same communication system architecture, with the fully activated
      RIS variant shown as a blue line with solid circles.

    The third baseline is the teacher-student learning framework [21] (green line with hol-

      low squares), serving as the primary competitor, with the fully activated RIS variant
      represented by a green line with hollow triangles. This comparison aims to evaluate
      differences in knowledge transfer mechanisms within dual-agent architectures.

5.2.1      Energy Efficiency

    “Energy efficiency” vs. “transmission power consumption” experimental results for ac-

tive/passive RIS are plotted in Fig. 5.1(a)/ 5.3(a). According to the results, as the transmission
power constraint is relaxed, the energy efficiency of all algorithms improves. The reason is that
the reduced energy constraint increases schedulable elements, resulting in a higher total trans-

mission rate. Observing Fig. 5.1(a), for the adjustable-switch active RIS scheme of this thesis’s
CDRL, the best EE performance is achieved owing to the adjustable switches and active RIS,

together with the catfish agent’s effective impetus for the main agent to explore strategies,
thereby learning superior beamforming and component-configuration combinations. The all-
on-switch active RIS scheme of this thesis’s CDRL exhibits lower EE performance because

when all elements are fully on, excessive element power consumption reduces overall energy
