4.2       Catfish-effect-based DRL training

       Since the energy efficiency problem discussed in chapter 3 is a non-convex optimiza-

tion problem, traditional mathematical methods require non-convex-to-convex transforma-
tions, resulting in computational complexity [11].         Compared to conventional alternat-
ing optimization (AO) methods, deep reinforcement learning (DRL) can optimize com-

plex dynamic problems without requiring prior mathematical relaxation [13]. This phase
provides an overview of the proposed CDRL model architecture and training methodol-

ogy.    The main agent is introduced in phase 4.2.1, including the formulation of states
spas act          pas act              pas act
